## Releasing

Releases are tag-driven. `release-manifest.json` is the single source of this
project's version — `config/settings.py` reads it via `get_project_version`, so
there is no `VERSION` file to keep in sync.

1. Update `release-manifest.json`: `version`, `summary`, `highlights`, and
   `release_url` (must be `https://github.com/{{ github_repo }}/releases/tag/vX.Y.Z`).
2. Add a matching `## vX.Y.Z` section to `CHANGELOG.md`.
3. `git tag -a vX.Y.Z -m "vX.Y.Z" && git push origin vX.Y.Z`

`.github/workflows/release.yml` then validates that the tag matches the manifest,
resolves the pinned DjangoLux version, builds amd64, and pushes
`{{ project_image }}:vX.Y.Z` plus `{{ project_image }}:{{ project_image_tag }}`,
baking both the baked-DjangoLux and release-manifest image labels. Composer reads
those labels to offer the update, and DjangoLux shows your summary and highlights
in the review dialog — so an image built without them shows no version or notes.

Do not hand-write `baked_dlux_version` into the manifest; the validator injects it
from the `requirements.txt` pin so the two can never disagree.

Requires repository secrets `DOCKERHUB_USERNAME` and `DOCKERHUB_TOKEN`.

## App Workflow

- Create new apps with `python -m dlux startapp app_name`
- Use `python -m dlux startapp app_name --register` if you want DjangoLux to patch `INSTALLED_APPS` and project URLs for you
- Generated apps follow the discovery-friendly Dlux layout: model-adjacent forms, filters, tables, views, translations, templates, and tests
- Keep app-local translations in `translations.py`
- Keep forms, filters, and tables in their conventional files so DjangoLux discovery can find them automatically

More project-specific guidance lives in [docs/README.md](docs/README.md).
